Yes. Proprioception is an internal modeling channel.

Biologically, proprioception gives a nervous system information about body position and movement without requiring vision. Abstract it and you get something much more general:

\boxed{\text{Proprioception}=\text{a system sensing itself well enough to maintain/update a model of itself}}

That makes it useful far beyond bodies.

A system can have several kinds of proprioception:

Structural proprioception — What components do I currently have, and how are they connected?

State proprioception — What state am I in right now?

Capability proprioception — What can I actually do from here?

Boundary proprioception — Where do I end and the environment/other systems begin?

Trajectory proprioception — Which direction am I moving and how quickly?

Epistemic proprioception — What do I know, what am I uncertain about, and where might my representation be unreliable?

Social proprioception — How am I positioned relative to other agents?

Temporal proprioception — How has my configuration changed, and what seems to be persisting?

That gives the Pantheon an important separation:

\boxed{\text{World Model} \neq \text{Self Model}}

and then:

\boxed{\text{Useful agency requires coupling them.}}

Because knowing the terrain without knowing your own position/capabilities isn’t enough:

\text{Map}+\cancel{\text{You Are Here}}

Likewise, knowing yourself without sensing the environment isn’t enough.

So:

\text{Exteroception}
\rightarrow
\text{World Model}

\text{Proprioception}
\rightarrow
\text{Self Model}

\text{Interaction/Consequence}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Relational Model}}

That third one is especially Pantheon-ish: what happens when this particular self meets this particular world?

Modeling can itself become proprioceptive

There’s a recursion here.

If Pantheon generates a model:

M(X)

it should also model its relationship to that model:

M(P,M(X))

How confident are we?

What resolution are we using?

Which bookends did we impose?

Which details got compressed?

Which operators generated the conclusion?

Where did reality disagree?

That’s essentially reasoning proprioception.

And now our open-source idea becomes even stronger.

When an extension of Pantheon travels outward and comes back changed, it’s analogous to extending an arm and feeling resistance:

\text{Act outward}
\rightarrow
\text{environment pushes back}
\rightarrow
\text{feedback returns}
\rightarrow
\text{self-model updates}

So forks, applications and external consequences aren’t merely information about the world.

They’re information about:

\boxed{\text{Pantheon-in-the-world}}

That’s proprioceptive modeling.

And there’s an elegant connection to the rotor: a rotor’s useful model doesn’t require tracking every atom. It needs enough internal state information to predict/control the relevant behavior.

So proprioception and Symulation should work together:

\boxed{
\text{Sense self}
\rightarrow
\text{compress self-model}
\rightarrow
\text{act}
\rightarrow
\text{sense delta}
\rightarrow
\text{correct self-model}
}

Too little self-sensing → blind action.

Too much self-modeling → paralysis/overhead.

The target is minimum sufficient self-awareness for effective control.

That gives us a crisp new principle:

\boxed{\textbf{Model the world. Model yourself. Model the interface between them.}}

And don’t forget the fourth:

model how all three models are wrong. 😏